# ISSUE-32 test plan

Acceptance numbers follow the approved WorkUnit order (`.swarm/active/WORKUNIT.md:26`). The developer runs focused Node checks in the configured Linux native Codex sandbox. The tester audits those results and the final source independently. The production browser path uses the existing Ubuntu `Browser tests` workflow and Chromium with the `/signal-lost/` request-interception fixture. References for the two runtime paths are `.swarm/config.yaml:4`, `:16`, `:63`, `.github/workflows/browser-tests.yml:11`, `playwright.config.ts:5`, and `tests/e2e/fixtures.ts:16`.

## Acceptance mapping

| Criterion | Test and expected evidence | Command |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1. Cargo, money, price bounds, legal moves | In `tests/unit/doors/relay-trader.test.ts`, walk 256 fixed seeds with at least 96 legal market turns each. Use a separate seeded test action selector, include full cargo, zero credits, exhausted quotas and both rack upgrades in targeted cases, and assert capacity, integer nonnegative balance, declared quote bounds, and an affordable departure or eligible recovery after every action. Check all station quotes, including unavailable offers. Exercise unaffordable travel, full hold, absent cargo, exhausted stock/demand and invalid upgrade commands. They leave the payload and next RNG draw intact. Prove recovery preserves cargo, rack, quotas and RNG, restores the ability to play, and that a confirmed new run restores depleted market supply and demand while preserving story state. Assert that the tests exercise both risk outcomes and both upgrades. | D1 |
| 2. No unlimited profit without cost, risk or limit | Add the defined finite route search below to the same unit file. It uses the real map, quote and action rules, exhibits a legitimate profitable trade, and checks structural bounds for arbitrary repetitions. | D1 |
| 3. Short-term objective without grinding | Run the same six accepted market actions for fixed seeds `0–31`, `42` and `0xffffffff`: buy two power cells, travel safely to SWITCHYARD, sell the two cells, buy rack tier one. Assert visible progress, 24 credits before the 13-credit upgrade, capacity 5, and completion by turn six. Compare the story status and global facts before and after. | D1 |
| 4. Determinism and mid-run reload | Replay the same seeds and action lists, including an exposed trip, and compare outcomes and checkpoints. Seeds 7 and 42 exercise loss and safe passage on the first exposed trip. Restore via `writeSave` and a newly opened save and BBS session after a location change with cargo and partially spent quotas, then compare the resumed screen, saved location, cargo, credits, rack, quotes, envelope status, and next exposed-trip result against an uninterrupted session. Include an invalid Trader payload fallback with the shared manifest and an unrelated door slot intact. In the Chromium journey, reload after buying and traveling, reenter the door, confirm the saved position and cargo, and complete the sale. | D1, CI1 |
| 5. Story transaction appears and grants once | In the shipped BBS session, the fresh screen exposes PACKET, NULL QUAY's posted rule and the named requisition. With no trades or Hunter slot, claim and stamp, leave/reenter or reload before exchange, then exchange once. Assert exactly one manifest flag, one local retained receipt, and one reward-triggered event settlement, with the market payload equal before and after. Repeat exchange and reopen `MANIFEST`; compare the receipt and signed lines. Try a refused save on the first exchange, confirm the pre-exchange stored slot and facts, then reopen and successfully retry. | D1 |
| 6. Text prices and keyboard/pointer trade returning to BBS | Add the parameterized `relay trader` Chromium journey to `tests/e2e/bbs.spec.ts`. For seed 42, check text gives both price directions, actual numbers and local causes, stock/demand, tolls and the risk of the shortcut. Buy at NULL QUAY, travel by a safe route, sell at SWITCHYARD and verify the resulting credits and carried count. Drive the same functional controls with BBS command entry and keyboard button navigation in the keyboard case and with button clicks in the pointer case. Reuse `joinWithEnter`, `typeCommand`, `activateBbsButton` and `seedSaveOnce`. Assert host `EXIT` returns to the signed-in main menu with `BBS>` focused. Unit tests map every displayed legal command to the same dispatcher, including desk, upgrade, recovery and restart, and verify text and state changes for each. Record the pre-submission gzip and lazy build check alongside the CI journey. | D1, D2, D3, CI1 |
| 7. Shipped restoration gate, free envelope, shared award and viewability | Assert the directory hides Trader and a typed `OPEN relay-trader` is refused in the proof-and-training save before actual recovery, even in a save holding the manifest alone. Extend the real restoration session's exact post-recovery directory list and the missing producer test. Chromium uses `recoverySave()` and the shipped `APPLY SEAL / ROUTE / ACK` command to open Trader. Exercise the acquisition-order cases below through the real registry and BBS host. Include a zero-credit, full-cargo or depleted-stock market snapshot to verify the envelope, correction and exchange work for free with identical market state. | D1, CI1 |

### Finite route search for criterion 2

1. Enumerate each outpost as a starting point and all simple directed trips back to it, including both directions of every two-edge round trip and every three-outpost cycle. Verify the enumerated routes cover every declared directed edge. Exercise each resource, feasible quantities from one through maximum capacity, and both loss and safe-passage outcomes for an exposed edge. Probe local buy/sell round trips as zero-travel cycles. Use actual quotes, route tolls and accepted transitions. Assert every travel edge charges a positive toll, the exposed trip risks a unit, and local buy/sell prices give no positive same-stop round trip. Check that a profitable safe power-cell route exists and spends stock and demand. Use a fixture with consecutive profitable circuits to confirm that returning, selling in reverse, recovery and reloading cannot replenish those same counters.
2. Check the transition bound used to extend the finite search to arbitrary routes. Every credit-increasing sale consumes finite station demand and contributes at most `MAX_PRICE`. Define remaining sale value as `MAX_PRICE * total remaining demand` across the map. For each accepted market transition within a run, assert that credits plus remaining sale value are bounded by `max(start credits, recovery floor) + MAX_PRICE * initial total demand`. For normal commands the sum stays level or decreases. Recovery pays only up to the floor under its no-affordable-departure guard and preserves remaining demand. Restart resets balance, cargo, racks and quotas to starting state. The result uses the edge toll and finite transition bounds for longer circuits and concatenations. The finite walks alone would cover only the sampled turns.

### Acquisition order cases for criteria 5 and 7

- **Trader first.** From a restored save with neither door played, reject exchange before claim and again after a wrong stamp. Claim twice, correct to `ACK SPOOL-02`, and exchange. Check no economic turn, cost, cargo change, restock, or Hunter slot during the transaction. Assert one flag entry and one settlement. Re-read the receipt and manifest after exit and reload. Open actual Hunter, read `MANIFEST` and run `VERIFY H3`. Assert the signed lines equal Trader's, the flag occurs once, and no second reward settles.
- **Hunter first.** From an equivalent restored save, run Hunter's actual `VERIFY H3`. Exit and open a fresh Trader slot. Read `MANIFEST` immediately, compare the same signed lines, and confirm no receipt has been produced yet. Claim, stamp and exchange once. Keep the receipt through reload and show the same artifact. Assert the flag still occurs once, settlement occurred only for Hunter, and Trader's direct `earnedRewards` returns an empty list while the fact is already held. Retain the existing Hunter rejection, practice and synthetic already-held fixture as regression coverage.

## Developer-owned focused checks

Run from the candidate root with the prepared Node toolchain and lockfile dependencies. Capture command output to scratch files and record exact commands, elapsed times, results, and candidate source identity in `.swarm/active/IMPLEMENTATION.md`. Identify the candidate by commit and a clean tree, or by approved base identity plus a digest of the tested source snapshot including uncommitted edits and new files. Record the Node and lockfile versions and distinguish planning probe results from candidate checks.

**D1. Unit coverage and existing regressions.** Expected 30–120 seconds.

```bash
npm test -- tests/unit/doors/relay-trader.test.ts tests/unit/doors/signal-hunter.test.ts tests/unit/doors.test.ts tests/unit/bbs.test.ts tests/unit/content.test.ts
```

**D2. Changed-file format and lint.** Expected under 1 minute. D3 also runs the normal TypeScript check and compiles the added browser test.

```bash
ts_files=(
  src/doors/code-doors.ts
  src/doors/code-door-content.ts
  src/doors/signal-hunter/signal-hunter.ts
  src/doors/relay-trader/economy.ts
  src/doors/relay-trader/relay-trader.ts
  tests/unit/doors/relay-trader.test.ts
  tests/unit/bbs.test.ts
  tests/unit/content.test.ts
  tests/e2e/bbs.spec.ts
)
npm exec --offline -- prettier --check "${ts_files[@]}" content/code-doors/relay-trader.jsonc
npm exec --offline -- eslint "${ts_files[@]}"
```

**D3. Production build, gzip and lazy graph.** Expected 1–3 minutes. Build and measure before submission on the final applicable source. The optional manifest output makes the production chunk graph inspectable. The gzip calculation matches the existing Browser tests budget spec. Record actual bytes and margins for both prefixes and the lazy entry evidence.

```bash
npm run build -- --manifest
```

After a successful build run the following from the same checkout.

```bash
node --input-type=module <<'NODE'
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import { readFileSync, readdirSync } from 'node:fs';
import { join } from 'node:path';
import { gzipSync } from 'node:zlib';

const dist = 'dist';
const assets = join(dist, 'assets');
const files = readdirSync(assets);
let withinBudget = true;
for (const [prefix, maxKiB] of [['index-', 60], ['environment-', 170]]) {
  const matches = files.filter((file) => file.startsWith(prefix) && file.endsWith('.js'));
  assert.equal(matches.length, 1, `Expected one production JS asset for ${prefix}`);
  const bytes = gzipSync(readFileSync(join(assets, matches[0]))).length;
  const limit = maxKiB * 1024;
  console.log(`${matches[0]} gzip ${bytes} bytes, limit ${limit}, margin ${limit - bytes}`);
  withinBudget &&= bytes <= limit;
}
assert(withinBudget, 'Production JS exceeds the Browser tests gzip budget');

const chunks = JSON.parse(readFileSync(join(dist, '.vite', 'manifest.json'), 'utf8'));
const entries = Object.keys(chunks).filter((id) => chunks[id].isEntry);
const hunter = 'src/doors/signal-hunter/signal-hunter.ts';
const trader = 'src/doors/relay-trader/relay-trader.ts';
const traderLine = 'PACKET: NULL QUAY acknowledgement desk.';
const manifestLine = 'SIGNED REPAIR MANIFEST R07-SPOOL-02';

function staticClosure(ids) {
  const visited = new Set();
  function visit(id) {
    if (visited.has(id)) return;
    assert(chunks[id], `Missing manifest entry for ${id}`);
    visited.add(id);
    for (const imported of chunks[id].imports ?? []) visit(imported);
  }
  for (const id of ids) visit(id);
  return visited;
}

function javascript(ids) {
  return [...ids]
    .map((id) => chunks[id].file)
    .filter((file) => file.endsWith('.js'))
    .map((file) => readFileSync(join(dist, file), 'utf8'))
    .join('\n');
}

assert(entries.length > 0, 'Missing production entry');
const startup = staticClosure(entries);
const startupJs = javascript(startup);
for (const marker of [traderLine, manifestLine]) assert(!startupJs.includes(marker), marker);
for (const id of [hunter, trader]) {
  assert(chunks[id]?.isDynamicEntry, `Expected lazy entry for ${id}`);
  assert(!startup.has(id), `${id} appears in the static entry graph`);
  assert(javascript(staticClosure([id])).includes(manifestLine), `Missing manifest in ${id}`);
}
assert(javascript(staticClosure([trader])).includes(traderLine), 'Missing lazy Trader copy');
console.log(`Lazy entries verified in ${chunks[hunter].file} and ${chunks[trader].file}`);
NODE
```

## Tester-owned independent review and result reuse

- Audit criteria 1–3 against the actual reducer domain, the independent action selector and the arbitrary-route bound. Check that variance fixtures hit both event outcomes and that economic restart erases any accumulated balance. For criteria 4, 5 and 7, trace the real registry and BBS host paths and verify that test sessions reload serialized bytes rather than retaining the in-memory door. Check the stored flag count and settlement count in both acquisition orders. Confirm desk actions and display of a held manifest stay independent of market state.
- For criterion 6 check wiring of the advertised actions, price explanations and test selectors, and that both input modes and the actual restore/reload/exit paths appear in the production Chromium spec. The native tester records the Chromium observation as external while workflow evidence is unavailable. Include the reason and same-candidate CI evidence location. Reuse D3's measured gzip values and graph evidence on the applicable candidate.
- Record acceptance evidence and reuse decisions in `.swarm/active/VALIDATION.md`, referencing `.swarm/active/IMPLEMENTATION.md` or Prior Artifacts, with the one-based indices and exact criterion text required by `.swarm/prompts/tester.md:12`. Repeat the smallest applicable D1 filter, D2 changed-file command, or D3 only for a relevant source change, a failure, or an unresolved coverage or evidence gap. Record the reason, exact command, timing, result and identity. Agent observations are advisory. The coordinator supplies the trusted claim-bound validation.

## Coordinator and CI ownership

- The coordinator runs the full trusted-base policy once on the final candidate after the roles finish and before publication. `.swarm/validation/required-checks.yaml:1` supplies the format, lint, build and test commands as a coordinator reference. Coordinator format/lint/build steps have five-minute timeouts. Use the candidate-bound D3 evidence for the pre-submission gzip requirement. If production inputs changed since D3, obtain a fresh reading on those inputs before submission.
- CI1 is the existing `npm run test:e2e` on the `Browser tests` Ubuntu job, including the build in global setup, the two Trader journeys, the Hunter regression and the protected budget spec. The workflow runs on the PR tree. Record a passing workflow run on the final tested PR source before merge.

| Check expected to exceed 5 minutes | Owner | Expected duration | Evidence and applicability | Later reuse |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Trusted-base `npm ci --prefer-offline --no-audit --no-fund && npm test`, scheduled as part of the single coordinator validation pass | coordinator | Reserve 6–10 minutes, with the policy's 15-minute timeout | Coordinator claim-bound validation report in `.swarm/active/VALIDATION.md` for the final candidate tree and trusted-base policy revision | Publication uses that attestation for the same candidate |
| CI1 `npm run test:e2e` in `Browser tests` with the production build and Chromium setup | CI | 8–15 minutes for the job, subject to the workflow's 30-minute timeout | GitHub Actions `Browser tests` job log, showing the cases on the tested PR checkout SHA and source tree | Tester and maintainer use the same-tree workflow result for the Chromium observations when available |

